The native adaptation proves the actual rank-two determinant argument
needed for point-ideal nonprincipality. It computes a representation in
the X-x,Y-y columns, constructs both multiplication-matrix factorizations
from actual ideal membership, and derives both polynomial norm factors.
Actual product degrees and the already proved Weierstrass norm degree-one
obstruction then exclude every proposed principal generator.

The full general Smith-coefficient, finite-dimensional module, and
finrank/norm theorem are not imported or assumed. They remain unported;
the explicit two-by-two argument proves the point-ideal conclusion needed
by the native class-group kernel argument directly. Full linear-basis
independence, nonsingular ideal inverse/addition identities, fractional
ideals/class groups and point-group injection remain separate obligations.

The actual noncubic model Y^2=X-x is also proved to have point ideal
principal(Y), exposing the material cubic premise in the obstruction.
